      "solution": "import sys\n\n\ndef escape_count(cr: float, ci: float, max_iter: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Number of iterations of z = z^2 + c (z0 = 0) done while |z|^2 <= 4, at most max_iter.\"\"\"\n    zr = zi = 0.0\n    n = 0\n    while n < max_iter and zr * zr + zi * zi <= 4.0:\n        zr, zi = zr * zr - zi * zi + cr, 2.0 * zr * zi + ci\n        n += 1\n    return n\n\n\ndef render(w, h, re_min, re_max, im_min, im_max, max_iter, palette):\n    inside = palette[-1]\n    bands = len(palette) - 1\n    rows = []\n    for r in range(h):\n        ci = im_max - r * (im_max - im_min) / (h - 1)\n        row = []\n        for c in range(w):\n            cr = re_min + c * (re_max - re_min) / (w - 1)\n            n = escape_count(cr, ci, max_iter)\n            row.append(inside if n == max_iter else palette[n % bands])\n        rows.append(\"\".join(row))\n    return rows\n\n\ndef main():\n    w, h = map(int, input().split())\n    re_min, re_max, im_min, im_max = map(float, input().split())\n    max_iter = int(input())\n    palette = input().strip()\n    print(\"\\n\".join(render(w, h, re_min, re_max, im_min, im_max, max_iter, palette)))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()",
      "statement": "The Mandelbrot set is famous for its endless detail: zoom anywhere on its border and new spirals, valleys and tiny copies of the whole set appear. Your goal is to explore it with a text console: given a rectangular <<box>> of the complex plane, draw it as <<ASCII art>>.\n\n<<The Mandelbrot set>>\nFor a complex number c, repeat z = z² + c starting from z = 0. The point c belongs to the set when z never escapes to infinity. In practice, it is enough to check whether |z|² = re(z)² + im(z)² becomes greater than {{4}}.\n\nWith z = x + yi and c = a + bi, one step is:\nx' = x² - y² + a\ny' = 2xy + b\n\n<<Escape count>>\nFor each point c, compute the escape count [[n]]:\n`n = 0, z = 0\nwhile n < maxIter and |z|² <= 4:\n    z = z² + c\n    n = n + 1`\nSo [[n]] is between 1 and [[maxIter]].\n\n<<From points to characters>>\nThe image has [[W]] columns and [[H]] rows. The character at column [[col]] (0 to [[W]]-1, left to right) and row [[row]] (0 to [[H]]-1, top to bottom) shows the point:\nre(c) = [[reMin]] + [[col]] × ([[reMax]] - [[reMin]]) / ([[W]] - 1)\nim(c) = [[imMax]] - [[row]] × ([[imMax]] - [[imMin]]) / ([[H]] - 1)\nThe four corners of the image are exactly the corners of the box, and the imaginary part grows upwards.\n\nWith a [[palette]] of [[L]] characters (indexed from 0):\n- if [[n]] = [[maxIter]], the point is considered inside the set: print the last character, [[palette]][ [[L]]-1 ];\n- otherwise, print [[palette]][ [[n]] mod ([[L]]-1) ].\nThe escaping points cycle through the first [[L]]-1 characters, which draws bands around the set.\n\n<<Example>>\nWith [[maxIter]] = {{10}} and [[palette]] = {{.:-=+*#%@}} ([[L]] = 9):\n- c = 1: z goes 0 → 1 → 2 → 5. After the 2nd step |z|² = 4, which is not greater than 4, so a 3rd step is done; then |z|² = 25 > 4. So [[n]] = 3 and the character is [[palette]][3 mod 8] = {{=}}.\n- c = -1: z goes 0 → -1 → 0 → -1 → ... and never escapes, so [[n]] = 10 = [[maxIter]] and the character is {{@}}.\n\nUse 64-bit floating-point numbers (double) and the formulas above. The test cases are chosen so that the result does not depend on rounding errors.\n\n<<Fun fact>>\nThis puzzle was inspired by a famous one-line C program that draws the set (it uses a slightly different algorithm, so it is not the specification):\n\n`main(n){float r,i,R,I,b;\nfor(i=-1;i<1;i+=.06,puts(\"\"))\nfor(r=-2;I=i,(R=r)<1;r+=.03,putchar(n+31))\nfor(n=0;b=I*I,26>n++&&R*R+b<4;\nI=2*R*I+i,R=R*R-b+r);}`\n\n<<References>>\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandelbrot_set\nhttps://people.sc.fsu.edu/~jburkardt/c_src/mandelbrot_ascii/mandelbrot_ascii.html\nhttp://paulbourke.net/dataformats/asciiart/",
